=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r8
diagnosis_text:
The OSPF virtual link between r4 (RID 14.1.4.1) and r8 (RID 18.1.4.1), traversing transit Area 0.0.0.1, is administratively up (Full/VLINK0 adjacency confirmed on both endpoints). However, after r8's ospfd process was restarted, the backbone database re-synchronisation over the virtual link was incomplete: r8's Area 0 LSDB contains 72 LSAs, while every other backbone router (r1, r2, r3, r4, r9) holds 73. The sole missing entry is the backbone ASBR-summary (Type-4) LSA for r11 (RID 112.1.1.1, checksum 0xea81), which r1 (RID 111.1.1.1) originates as the ABR for Area 3. r4 holds this LSA in its backbone LSDB and should have flooded it to r8 over VLINK0 during the DD exchange, but it was never delivered. r11 is the ASBR redistributing EIGRP routes (112.0.0.0/8, 103.0.0.0/8 plus connected prefixes) into OSPF as 11 Type-5 external LSAs. Without the Type-4, FRR's external-route computation on r8 cannot resolve the forwarding path to the ASBR and discards all 11 Type-5 entries from the RIB. Consequence: from r8 (and Area 2), every external destination — 102.1.x.0/24, 103.0.0.0/8, 112.x.x.x, 120.x.x.x — returns "Network is unreachable", while all internal OSPF prefixes remain reachable. The backbone can reach Area 2 normally. The root cause is the OSPF virtual link's failure to deliver the backbone Type-4 ASBR-summary to r8 during post-restart database re-synchronisation on r8. Corrective fix: on r4, execute `vtysh -c "clear ip ospf process"` to tear down and re-establish the virtual link; during the new full DD exchange r4 will re-flood all backbone LSAs including the Type-4 to r8. Verify success with `show ip ospf database asbr-summary` on r8 (backbone section must be non-empty) and confirm `ping 102.1.1.1` from r8 succeeds.
=== END ===